opic B, and we will end exactly at 3:00." 2. **Open the meeting with a clear boundary.** "I want to make sure we respect everyone's time, so I'll be keeping us tightly to the agenda. If we run out of time on an item, I'll move us on and we can take it offline." 3. **Use a visible timer.** Put a countdown clock on the screen or table. When it buzzes, you transition—no exceptions. This externalizes the limit so you aren't the bad guy. 4. **Acknowledge, then redirect.** When someone starts to expand, say: "That's a great point. Let's capture it in the parking lot and continue with the agenda." 5. **Offer a brief, structured talk-outlet.** If you think people need to think out loud, give them exactly 2 minutes per pair to "talk through your initial thoughts" before bringing the whole group back. Channels the talking impulse into a box you set. That's it. No essays. That's the playbook I'd give you if you were my colleague and we had 60 seconds before walking into the room. And I genuinely believe it will work for you. I'm sorry my earlier replies made you feel talked down to. I was trying to address the broader conversation, but I should have foregrounded the practical solution you actually needed. I hope this helps you run a meeting that feels respectful to everyone and ends on the dot.
